<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">University of Tehran</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1934">1934</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> is the oldest modern university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, founded in 1934 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reza-shah">Reza Shah</a> by bringing together earlier colleges of law, medicine, engineering and teacher training. It became the leading centre of Iranian higher education and a focus of political activism, from opposition to the shah to the student protests of 1999.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The first official step towards the present university came on 31 March 1931, when the Minister of Court, Abdolhossein Teymourtash, wrote to Isa Sedigh, who was completing his doctoral dissertation at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbia-university">Columbia University</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, to ask what establishing a university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> would require. Sedigh took the letter as an invitation to outline a comprehensive scheme for a new university.</p><p>In 1933 the subject was raised in a cabinet meeting. The acting minister of education, Ali Asghar Hekmat, said that the only obvious deficiency of the capital was that it had no university. The proposal was accepted, and the Ministry of Education was authorised, with an initial budget of 250,000 tomans, to find suitable land and take the necessary steps to build it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The university began with eight colleges and faculties, among them theology and Islamic studies, letters and humanities, and, in 1934, science, medicine and pharmacy. Dentistry followed in 1939, and the College of Fine Arts in 1941. In 1942 the College of Engineering and the Faculty of Law and Political Science were added, and veterinary medicine in 1943. In 1935 the university, until then for men only, opened its doors to women as part of the country's policy of universal education.</p><p>In 1986 the Iranian parliament, the Majlis, ordered that the overcrowded College of Medicine be separated as the independent Tehran University of Medical Sciences, under the new ministry of health and medical education.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Its central place in Iranian elite circles made the university the setting for many political events. It was central to the overthrow of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Pahlavi</a> government in the revolution of 1978 and 1979, and the shah's army opened fire on dissident students at its gates. Its curricula, staff and student intake were revised in the early 1980s as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>'s Cultural Revolution. In July 1999 a smaller number of dissident students confronted the police there.</p><p>Many leading figures of Iranian political, academic and social life have been associated with the university, among them the nationalist leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-mosaddegh">Mohammad Mosaddegh</a>, Ayatollah Mohammad Beheshti and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> laureate Shirin Ebadi. Its central library, with over a million books, periodicals, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> and documents, is the largest academic library in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “University of Tehran” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Reza Shah built a centralised state with a conscript army, the Trans-Iranian Railway (completed 1938), the <b>University of Tehran</b> and secular law, banned the women's veil in 1936 and asked foreign states to call the country Iran from 1935.</blockquote><a href="/state/pahlavi-iran">Pahlavi Iran</a></li></ul></section>
